WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:06.520
Many times when you look at a very successful
business from the outside, it looks like everything's

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:07.520
going perfect.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.740
Like there should be zero problems.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:15.880
But, in order for a company to become successful,
they've got to do the ugly things very good,

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:20.960
behind closed doors that nobody sees, and
one of the ugliest things you'll ever have

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.339
to do, as a leader, as a boss, as a CEO, as
a C-type executive, whoever it is that's running

00:00:27.339 --> 00:00:29.619
the department, is firing people.

00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:34.600
And today I'm going to talk to you about how
to fire a teammate, someone, or an employee,

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:35.600
part of your company.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.972
And before I get into anything I'm going to
cover here with you, I want you to keep a

00:00:37.972 --> 00:00:39.440
couple of things in mind.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.620
You've got to follow your state, and your
country's human resources, HR guidelines.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.690
So whatever I tell you, your laws go first.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.200
These are just simple philosophies on things
that can help with your business.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.720
Look, I've been around the block for a while.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.480
I've hired and fired so many different people,
and the first time's always a difficult one.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.190
It's very similar to, the closest correlation
I can give you on what it is to fire somebody

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.590
is when you're breaking up with somebody.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:13.290
It's always good if somebody has broken up
with someone and someone else has broken up

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.290
with you.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.470
If you have experience on both sides, you
will know when somebody broke up with you,

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:19.470
it hurt.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:23.050
And you will know when you broke up with somebody,
what their reaction was.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:29.210
And there really is a general consensus to
how things should be, the goal is you want

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.750
to finish things amicably, right?

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.340
So, you know the typical breakup - what's
the famous breakup line?

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.820
We all know it.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.840
Honey, it's really not you, it's me.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.010
I'm the problem.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.700
We hear something that says, I just don't
see this thing going the direction we want

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.700
it to go.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.460
Hey, we both knew this day was coming, or
whatever it is.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.500
We have all these breakup lines.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:56.430
Firing has also got some lines, but I think
it's very important to know you're dealing

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.610
with a human being and if the first thing
you think about is you're dealing with a human

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:03.390
being, you'll take the approach in a completely
different way then looking at someone as let

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.450
me just fire this person because it's robotic.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.840
You're still dealing with a human being.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.000
So 11 points I'll cover with you that will
help you out.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.400
Before I get into that as well, one of the
biggest reasons why you have to put yourself

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:23.209
in the position to fire somebody is because
you didn't properly hire somebody.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.049
So what do I mean you didn't properly hire
somebody?

00:02:25.049 --> 00:02:27.060
Why don't we talk about that first?

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.750
So Pat, what do you mean we didn't hire someone
properly?

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.540
Did you call references?

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.569
What type of references did you call when
you spoke to them?

00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:39.080
Did they go and talk to some other people
to see if their personality is going to fit

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.330
the culture?

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:44.329
Did you just hire them because you liked them
a lot, but they have zero skill set?

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:48.300
Did you do a background check on them to see
about anything that they have going on?

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.489
Did you ask the proper question when they
say, "Oh, I've taken a sabbatical for two

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:53.489
years and I haven't worked."

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:58.450
Is that really the case or is that a script
some people say when for the last two years

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:02.280
they have had a job, but they didn't finish
well and didn't want you to call the job because

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.700
you don't want the reference.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:04.879
Maybe they didn't quit the job.

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:05.879
Maybe they were fired.

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:07.829
Maybe they have some bad things they don't
want to tell you about.

00:03:07.829 --> 00:03:10.799
Anytime somebody's got a long gap in work,
there's a concern.

00:03:10.799 --> 00:03:11.890
There's a red flag.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.900
So you've got to ask those types of questions.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.329
And once you ask those types of questions,
then you hire.

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:21.700
Sometimes you also try to force someone's
talent into a job that they're not a part

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:22.700
of.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:23.700
I'll cover that as well.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.709
You've got to figure out who the person is,
do they fit the position, then you hire them.

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:32.290
So, above anything, the rule that I live by
when it comes to hiring and firing is hire

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:36.400
very slow, fire extremely fast.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.860
Hire very slow, fire extremely fast.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:42.980
There's nothing worse than a boyfriend and
a girlfriend being in a relationship that

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:48.510
they know is never, ever going to work out,
but they just keep dragging it out and it

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.639
just keeps wasting both of the people's lives.

00:03:50.639 --> 00:03:55.049
I've done it, many people do it, there's no
need for it, when it comes to business.

00:03:55.049 --> 00:03:57.599
So let's get into the 11 different points
that I have.

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:01.989
#1: First things first when it comes time
to fire somebody.

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:04.879
The goal is to fire them gently.

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:05.999
Period.

00:04:05.999 --> 00:04:07.430
The goal is to fire them gently.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:10.040
What does this mean, fire them gently?

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:17.030
They didn't work out, they leave, you're happy,
they're happy, no more I can't believe this

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:21.290
is what happened, I can't believe that is
what happened - your goal is to fire gently.

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:26.810
There doesn't need to be any kind of retaliation
on both sides because the employer can obviously

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:30.810
hurt the employee, and the employee can obviously
hurt the employer.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.450
So if anybody starts it, both ways it's not
going to be pretty, and it's not going to

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:35.640
help the career.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:36.850
Why is it not going to help the career?

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:41.230
Because if the employer bashes the employee,
that means new employees don't want to work

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.270
for the employer.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.730
Because everybody that works for this company
if you leave, they're going to bash you.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:49.110
And the employee doesn't want to bash the
employer because no matter what other place

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.110
you go to, one of the worst things I like
to hear in an interview is what?

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:57.780
When somebody is sitting there and I'm interviewing
them, and the employee keeps bashing the prior

00:04:57.780 --> 00:04:59.650
two companies they worked for.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:01.480
The companies are not the problem.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.450
Generally, it's the person who's the problem.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.290
It's not necessarily always the company.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:08.470
So if the person says, "Oh, my last boss was
this.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:11.530
My last boss was this" - we already see a
sign of that.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:15.420
So as somebody that's hired a lot of people,
that's a red flag, when I'm looking at hiring

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.420
somebody.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:17.670
So the goal is to let people go gently.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.750
Look, I have a daughter.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:21.890
One day, my daughter's going to date a man.

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:25.340
And they're going to go on their first date,
and she's going to have a boyfriend, okay?

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:29.060
Now my daughter right now is four months old,
so that's not coming for at least forty more

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:30.060
years, right?

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.120
She's going to take her time for going and
having a boyfriend.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:39.150
But when that day comes, and she has a boyfriend,
and then there's a breakup, as a father, and

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:44.900
my personality is extremely over protective
and I have access to the resources, to money,

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.560
I know people from all over the place, there's
a lot of different resources that I have access

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:49.560
to.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.800
My goal is for that breakup to be what?

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:56.790
To be as amicable as possible, and for the
boyfriend to not go bashing and saying things,

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.320
and my daughter to not do either.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:05:59.320
None of them.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.980
Hey, if it didn't work out, it didn't work
out, you guys go your separate ways.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:07.340
That's your outcome, with 99.9% of the people
you fire, that's your outcome.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.610
Now, what's the .1%?

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:15.000
The .1%, this person breached the contract
and they want to go out there and do something

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.480
that's going to hurt the company?

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:22.300
Well, it's unfortunate, but this is what general
counsel and legal counsel comes in, and I

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:23.300
won't get into it.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:24.730
I've been on both sides of the aisle myself.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:29.500
I know exactly what it is to be on both sides,
so I know the experiences, but that's the

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:30.500
.1%.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:34.353
And when the .1% takes place, you've got to
make sure you do something that will protect

00:06:34.353 --> 00:06:37.840
your company that you worked very hard for,
if somebody decides to go that route.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:42.470
But that rarely happens, very, very rarely
does that ever happen.

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:45.530
Point #2 - when you're firing somebody, get
to the point.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.100
Don't drag it out.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.590
There's nothing more annoying than a drag
out breakup.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:52.060
Hey, this is not working out, here's why.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:57.140
I want to get right into it, I don't like
firing anybody, this is the part of the business

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.200
I don't like, but this is just not working
out.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.640
[snap], to the point, okay?

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:06.940
And as you're going through it, there may
be a part, a shock factor to the person, [Mario,

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:11.900
I'm not pointing at you because we're firing
you, I'm just looking at you here.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:15.050
. . ] there's a shock factor to somebody you
fire.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:20.200
So, you know the shock factor is oh my gosh,
what do you mean?

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:24.340
So there may be the opportunity for them to
say, "But I thought this.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:25.340
I thought it was working out.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:26.660
I thought this."

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:30.460
You don't need to defend yourself because
if you're firing somebody, when it gets to

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.780
that point, you have already done your proper
homework.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:36.070
Especially after watching this video, where
you're not firing because you're winging it.

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.720
You're firing because there's a lot of other
things you're going to do before you fire

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:39.720
somebody.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:40.720
So you need to stay firm.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:43.130
#3: The next point is stay firm, as you're
firing somebody.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.860
So gently, get to the point, stay firm.

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.590
Don't defend why you're firing.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:50.340
Don't defend, you know, constantly with them
going back and forth.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.650
This is not a debate.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:52.810
This is a decision.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:54.080
We're not debating here.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:56.890
This is the decision that's been made, you're
fired.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:59.840
You need to move on, and this just didn't
work out.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:03.210
Gentle, clear, to the point, firm.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:06.680
#4: and then after that part, acknowledge
their feelings.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:11.650
Listen, I understand that this may be a bit
of a frustrating moment for you.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:14.080
I understand this may be a disappointing moment
for you.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.990
I've been fired before and I can tell you,
it's not very exciting when this happens.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:21.490
However, I just want to make sure I totally
understand your feelings and what you're thinking

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:22.490
about.

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:29.820
#5: Next, typically, the protocol I like to
follow is, exit interview is always done by

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.490
the person who hired them.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:35.689
If I hired Mario, I'm firing Mario.

00:08:35.689 --> 00:08:38.479
You don't give your dirty work to somebody
else.

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:42.810
If you hired somebody, and you were the person
that said, We have made a decision to give

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:46.610
you the position, you need to make sure you're
doing the firing.

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:53.100
Now let's just say if you did the hiring,
but they worked under John, who runs that

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:58.139
entire department, then it's you and John
together in the room, talking to the person

00:08:58.139 --> 00:08:59.589
that's getting fired.

00:08:59.589 --> 00:09:00.589
Both people.

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:02.610
But there needs to be an exit interview.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:07.930
Sometimes people do firing in ways that just,
you know you just got to do it and take care

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.820
of it and this is not a way to hurt somebody.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:12.139
You want to make sure it's done in a proper
way.

00:09:12.139 --> 00:09:18.680
If you're dealing with somebody who's a vendor
or somebody who is an independent contractor,

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:21.279
that's helping you out with your IT services
and you're firing them, obviously, you're

00:09:21.279 --> 00:09:25.029
going to fire them over the phone, or something
like that.

00:09:25.029 --> 00:09:28.910
But if you're dealing with somebody that's
in house team, you're doing it face-to-face.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:33.690
#6: Generally, it also shouldn't be a surprise.

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:35.040
This is point #6.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.040
It shouldn't be a surprise.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:38.519
Let me explain what I mean by that.

00:09:38.519 --> 00:09:41.769
Generally it shouldn't be a surprise, and
here's why it shouldn't be a surprise.

00:09:41.769 --> 00:09:45.709
It shouldn't be a surprise because throughout
the process, let's just say if Bob has worked

00:09:45.709 --> 00:09:48.360
with me for 60 days.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:53.569
And within the first week, two weeks, three
weeks, four weeks, I will generally know areas

00:09:53.569 --> 00:09:55.129
that I'm not happy with Bob.

00:09:55.129 --> 00:10:00.370
And I'll say, "Bob, this is very important,
and this was part of your, what we hired,

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:05.850
when we hired you, on the application, it
explained your job description.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.690
I want to highlight, this is part of your
job description.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:12.949
So I'm going back to the job description that
I put up - important - if you don't have a

00:10:12.949 --> 00:10:16.579
clear job description up front, then they
can come back and say, "Well, you never told

00:10:16.579 --> 00:10:18.399
me this was part of my job description."

00:10:18.399 --> 00:10:19.760
So they're held accountable to what?

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:20.760
Their job description.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.889
You can tell the job description here and
you're not doing this right.

00:10:23.889 --> 00:10:27.579
You told me you type 65 words per minute,
but it looks like you're typing 35 words per

00:10:27.579 --> 00:10:28.579
minute.

00:10:28.579 --> 00:10:31.079
You told me your spelling is right, but there's
seven, eight different spelling errors here

00:10:31.079 --> 00:10:33.709
in this email you're sending to a customer.

00:10:33.709 --> 00:10:34.709
What happened here?

00:10:34.709 --> 00:10:35.709
Tell me about it.

00:10:35.709 --> 00:10:36.709
Do you really know?

00:10:36.709 --> 00:10:37.709
Okay, here's what I want you to do.

00:10:37.709 --> 00:10:40.220
Why don't you write this out, or whatever,
you're doing some testing, you're doing some

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.360
accountability and coaching, right?

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:47.000
So if it happens again, another time, and
you say, "Bob, this really needs to improve.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.389
If it doesn't, it's part of your job description.

00:10:50.389 --> 00:10:51.589
If it happens again.

00:10:51.589 --> 00:10:55.579
. ." or "Bob, you told me you're somebody
that shows up on time, three times in the

00:10:55.579 --> 00:10:57.399
last two weeks, you've been late.

00:10:57.399 --> 00:10:59.209
"But it's only eight minutes."

00:10:59.209 --> 00:11:01.399
Eight minutes is eight minutes.

00:11:01.399 --> 00:11:05.689
Eight minutes is a long time, but you're still
late three times in the last two weeks, and

00:11:05.689 --> 00:11:08.730
you said you're punctual and we're expecting
people that are here punctual.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:11.680
I just want you to know, if that continues,
that's going to be a challenge.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:15.149
So let's just say those two things continue,
right?

00:11:15.149 --> 00:11:19.389
When it comes down to firing them, they're
not sitting there surprised, because you're

00:11:19.389 --> 00:11:24.140
going to say, Bob, I don't think this is going
to be a surprise to you, because you know,

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:26.920
when you got started, you were late three
times in two weeks, and I told you that's

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:27.920
an issue.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:31.559
And this has happened again three more times
in the last four weeks, so we just have to

00:11:31.559 --> 00:11:32.890
let you go.

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:33.890
Boom, to the point.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:35.040
See how easy that was?

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:36.040
You're getting to the point.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:39.620
Hey, look, one of the job descriptions, and
I brought this up to you several times was

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:40.910
you need to be working faster.

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:45.059
You told me you work in a very pressured type
situation, but it seems like a couple times

00:11:45.059 --> 00:11:48.670
when it was you hitting deadlines, you didn't
perform well during deadlines, and this happened

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:49.709
on three different occasions.

00:11:49.709 --> 00:11:51.999
And that's very important to us.

00:11:51.999 --> 00:11:53.709
And we just have to let you know, we have
to let you go.

00:11:53.709 --> 00:11:56.480
And I'm sure it's not a surprise, because
this has been brought up.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:00.819
So if you do it properly, while you're going
through the probationary period and you're

00:12:00.819 --> 00:12:04.339
telling them areas they need to improve in,
there's not going to be a surprise when it

00:12:04.339 --> 00:12:05.339
happens.

00:12:05.339 --> 00:12:09.769
And many times, they'll even know that it's
getting to a point that this may not work

00:12:09.769 --> 00:12:12.029
out, and many times they'll quit before you're
going to fire them.

00:12:12.029 --> 00:12:13.290
It's the same thing.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:18.050
If you do it proper with accountability, it
will get to the point where they'll say, "You

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:19.050
know what?

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:20.459
Honestly, I just don't think I fit.

00:12:20.459 --> 00:12:24.990
I'm just really trying and really doing this,
it's just not going to work for me."

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:25.990
No problem.

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:26.990
I totally understand.

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:27.990
Boom.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:28.990
All good, we let you go.

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:29.990
It's a lot easier.

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:32.029
Sometimes you want the other person to break
up with you, right, because you don't want

00:12:32.029 --> 00:12:33.029
to break their heart.

00:12:33.029 --> 00:12:36.769
And sometimes when the other girl or guy comes
and says, I just can't see this going long,

00:12:36.769 --> 00:12:40.600
and in the back of your mind you're saying,
thank God she just said it.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:43.220
You're right, I wish it would, but you're
right.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:44.220
Let's just move on.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:45.220
I agree.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:46.220
So that happens as well.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:50.439
So that happens as well, many times when you're
going through that.

00:12:50.439 --> 00:12:51.869
So that area's covered.

