. . okay, perfect.

00:12:53.009 --> 00:12:54.350
#7: So next one.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:58.509
The next one is as you're doing the exit interview,
here's what I like to ask.

00:12:58.509 --> 00:13:04.649
I used to, thirteen years ago, get right into
giving feedback on why I'm firing.

00:13:04.649 --> 00:13:06.250
Some people don't want it.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:07.290
Some people don't want it.

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:08.519
And they're offended.

00:13:08.519 --> 00:13:09.769
Now, I ask.

00:13:09.769 --> 00:13:12.009
Do I have your permission to give you some
feedback?

00:13:12.009 --> 00:13:13.009
This is the exit interview.

00:13:13.009 --> 00:13:14.029
Would you like some feedback?

00:13:14.029 --> 00:13:17.769
Yes, I would like to get some feedback from
you.

00:13:17.769 --> 00:13:22.569
When you are giving feedback, I cover feedback
in a couple of different ways.

00:13:22.569 --> 00:13:27.589
And my feedback is extremely intentional because
I have one outcome with me letting you go.

00:13:27.589 --> 00:13:32.680
My goal is for you to leave me and do 10 times
better than you did while you were with me.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:33.680
That's my goal.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:37.879
When I let an employee go and it didn't work
out, I want you to go be successful.

00:13:37.879 --> 00:13:39.259
I want you to go out there and make money.

00:13:39.259 --> 00:13:40.339
I don't want you to stay on unemployment.

00:13:40.339 --> 00:13:42.449
I want you to go figure out a way to make
money.

00:13:42.449 --> 00:13:44.430
So I'll say, "Look, there's a couple things
I want you to know about.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:47.999
One of the things I want you to know about
is it just seems like you're always late.

00:13:47.999 --> 00:13:52.139
Second thing I want you to know about is it
seems that you don't follow details, and details

00:13:52.139 --> 00:13:53.160
is not your game.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:57.800
The third thing is your customer service needs
some work, the way you talk to people.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:04.149
If you're planning on being in a part of business
where you're touching the customer, that's

00:14:04.149 --> 00:14:05.269
got to improve.

00:14:05.269 --> 00:14:08.699
So you may need to be the a behind the scenes
type of a person, that you're not touching

00:14:08.699 --> 00:14:09.699
the customer.

00:14:09.699 --> 00:14:10.699
That's okay.

00:14:10.699 --> 00:14:11.699
They don't need to see you.

00:14:11.699 --> 00:14:14.160
But if you're going to be touching customers,
that's got to improve.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:17.480
Or it could be another thing where, look,
if you don't know something, don't tell people

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:18.480
you know it.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:22.470
Three times you told me you know it and you
didn't it know it, and you lost credibility

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:23.470
with me.

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.790
In the future, I'm a lot more comfortable
with the person telling me, Patrick, I honestly

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:29.300
don't know, but can you give me a date to
learn it by?

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:30.309
I like that response.

00:14:30.309 --> 00:14:33.079
Why don't you use that response in your next
company?

00:14:33.079 --> 00:14:34.870
That's a great idea, and they'll take notes.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:35.870
Perfect.

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:36.929
Any questions you have for me?

00:14:36.929 --> 00:14:37.929
Boom.

00:14:37.929 --> 00:14:39.149
But you're giving feedback there.

00:14:39.149 --> 00:14:42.779
And you talk about their strengths, because
there isn't anybody you'll ever find that

00:14:42.779 --> 00:14:43.779
does not have strengths.

00:14:43.779 --> 00:14:46.370
So when you say that you can't really think
of anything positive to say about the person

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:52.949
- everybody, everybody I've ever fired, they've
always had strengths.

00:14:52.949 --> 00:14:55.709
It just didn't work out for many different
reasons.

00:14:55.709 --> 00:14:59.199
So you do want to highlight their strengths
that they have as well.

00:14:59.199 --> 00:15:02.010
I remember one time I had an employee.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:05.149
This was what year, Mario?

00:15:05.149 --> 00:15:06.540
'05, '06.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:07.959
I might give this person a nickname.

00:15:07.959 --> 00:15:09.360
The right people know his nickname.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:10.670
His nickname is Hoot.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:13.490
And he loves the fact that I'm mentioning
his name in this video.

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:15.970
I won't give his full name.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:19.279
First of all, I loved this guy.

00:15:19.279 --> 00:15:27.079
He is probably one of my, if not my favorite
assistant of all time, who at the same time

00:15:27.079 --> 00:15:32.051
was by far probably the worst assistant in
the history of assistants.

00:15:32.051 --> 00:15:34.380
This guy was terrible.

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:35.589
He was horrible.

00:15:35.589 --> 00:15:37.029
And I dragged it out for eight months.

00:15:37.029 --> 00:15:40.420
I think it's more because I loved him, he's
like my younger brother.

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:41.970
And he was terrible at it!

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:43.470
I mean, things would never get done.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:44.839
He was never organized.

00:15:44.839 --> 00:15:48.300
I was telling him what to do every single
day, instead of the relationship being the

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:49.519
other way around.

00:15:49.519 --> 00:15:54.420
But he liked to, you know, be around my cars,
we'd hang out, he would listen to this stuff,

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:57.489
we would drive in the cars, we'd go places
together so it was always good.

00:15:57.489 --> 00:16:00.420
He was very good at making sure the oil change
was done properly.

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:02.900
He was very, very good in that part of it.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:07.749
And one day I sat him down and I said, "Hoot,
I've got to tell you this, man," and he knew

00:16:07.749 --> 00:16:08.749
it was coming.

00:16:08.749 --> 00:16:10.759
"Hoot, buddy, I've got good news and I've
got bad news.

00:16:10.759 --> 00:16:11.759
What do you want to know?"

00:16:11.759 --> 00:16:15.069
He said, "Pat, I know what it is, but tell
me the bad news."

00:16:15.069 --> 00:16:17.819
The bad news is you are terrible.

00:16:17.819 --> 00:16:20.579
My gosh, you are terrible at being an assistant.

00:16:20.579 --> 00:16:22.100
He said, "What's the good news?"

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:25.059
I said, "You have no idea how much I trust
you.

00:16:25.059 --> 00:16:26.059
I trust you.

00:16:26.059 --> 00:16:28.999
You are such a kind human being.

00:16:28.999 --> 00:16:30.920
People love being around you.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:33.180
But you're not a good assistant.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:34.759
And I will tell you this.

00:16:34.759 --> 00:16:39.839
Sometimes, people who don't make good assistants,
they make very good entrepreneurs.

00:16:39.839 --> 00:16:42.550
They make very good independent contractors.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:44.939
They make very good in a completely different
area.

00:16:44.939 --> 00:16:45.940
So this is not a bad thing.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:47.779
But what do you want to do with your life?

00:16:47.779 --> 00:16:49.279
And we went through a conversation together.

00:16:49.279 --> 00:16:51.619
I said, "Listen, tell me what you want to
do with your life?"

00:16:51.619 --> 00:16:52.910
He said, "Pat, honestly, I love surfing."

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:54.519
I said, "Okay, so what's your point with surfing?

00:16:54.519 --> 00:16:55.519
Many people love surfing."

00:16:55.519 --> 00:16:56.519
He said, "No.

00:16:56.519 --> 00:16:57.519
I REALLY love surfing."

00:16:57.519 --> 00:17:00.959
I said, "Okay, tell me what you really love
about surfing" and he went into it.

00:17:00.959 --> 00:17:04.920
I said, "So why don't you go travel the world
and go surf all the places you want to surf?

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:06.160
You're 21 years old."

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:08.199
He said, "You think I can really do that?"

00:17:08.199 --> 00:17:10.640
I said, "Yeah, go work all over the place,
and go surf.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:12.000
What places do you want to surf?"

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.610
I want to surf in this place, I want to surf
in New Zealand, I want to surf in Australia.

00:17:15.610 --> 00:17:17.249
I want to surf in.

00:17:17.249 --> 00:17:19.640
. . He went through all these places in the
world he wants to surf.

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:21.720
Do you know what he did for the next 10 years?

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:23.529
He went and surfed at all these places!

00:17:23.529 --> 00:17:26.110
He literally lives there.

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:27.730
He lived in all of these places.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:30.730
China, some strange places in the world, and
he went surfing.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:32.510
And you know how he made his money?

00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:36.769
He made his money teaching other people how
to surf at the beach, while he was surfing.

00:17:36.769 --> 00:17:39.980
So while he was teaching other people how
to surf, and working at restaurants, he was

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:40.980
surfing.

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:42.090
And he got that out of his system.

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:46.330
But I'm so happy for him because he was able
to go and pursue what he wanted to do with

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:47.330
his life.

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:49.070
He was a terrible assistant.

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:54.140
Till today, very good relationship, he comes
and sees me or my kids, he can spend the night

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:56.610
at our house, he's like family to me.

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:59.409
And he's probably watching this right now,
cracking up, and he'll be one of the first

00:17:59.409 --> 00:18:00.510
to comment on this.

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:02.289
Hoot, much love to you.

00:18:02.289 --> 00:18:04.940
Much, much love to you from me and everybody
else to you.

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:06.260
#8:Don't drag it out.

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:09.530
Do not drag out firing somebody.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:12.201
If you're going to fire somebody, and you
already know that they need to be fired, don't

00:18:12.201 --> 00:18:13.360
wait two more months.

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:14.590
Don't wait four more months.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:16.700
It's probably not going to change.

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:21.809
Things that are very, very important for that
job to be done, it's probably not going to

00:18:21.809 --> 00:18:22.809
change.

00:18:22.809 --> 00:18:23.809
You need to fire them.

00:18:23.809 --> 00:18:27.120
You need to fire them now is what you need
to do, if there are things that you don't

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:29.190
necessarily see improving a lot.

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:31.840
But some of you are going to watch this and
you're going to say, "I have to go fire these

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:33.049
three people!"

00:18:33.049 --> 00:18:34.049
Wait.

00:18:34.049 --> 00:18:39.731
Have you given them already the feedback on
those areas you need to improve, and have

00:18:39.731 --> 00:18:43.740
you given them two, four, six weeks to see
if there's going to be progress on it.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:47.539
If you have gone through all of these, then
fire them.

00:18:47.539 --> 00:18:50.113
Don't get off this video and just say I'm
going to be on this firing one, I'm going

00:18:50.113 --> 00:18:51.919
to fire every single person I see in my office.

00:18:51.919 --> 00:18:54.370
Then you'll have nobody left and you'll go
out of business.

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:55.510
You don't want to do that.

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:59.120
But once you realize who it is, you've got
to make sure you don't drag this entire process

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:00.120
out.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:03.610
#9: I already told you kind of earlier, hire
slow, fire fast.

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:10.270
#10: Always when you hire people, have a 120
day probationary period, sometimes 90 days,

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:14.630
sometimes 120 days, which means you're getting
an opportunity to work with us for 90 days

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:18.390
. We will decide within the next 90 days if
you'll stay with us, but this is a probationary

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:19.390
period.

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:21.080
At any point we could decide to go a different
direction.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.190
Are you comfortable with that?

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:23.190
Yes.

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:24.190
Perfect.

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:25.190
Come on board.

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:26.190
And we give them their 90 days.

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:27.190
Your 90 days is going to be August 1st.

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:28.190
Your 90 days is going to be July 1st.

00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:30.850
We just want you to know that during these
90 days we're going to learn a lot about each

00:19:30.850 --> 00:19:31.850
other.

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:32.850
Are you comfortable with that?

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:33.850
Yes.

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:34.850
Perfect.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:40.169
So then, while you're firing, you've got to
also be prepared to have a plan for replacement.

00:19:40.169 --> 00:19:44.330
You need to replace this person that is no
longer with you.

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:48.320
So there needs to be a plan of replacement.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:53.440
Sometimes you can do it properly, with that
person that's going to be leaving trains the

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:54.440
replacement.

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:56.270
They train the replacement.

00:19:56.270 --> 00:19:58.820
And there's nothing like that if you do it
properly.

00:19:58.820 --> 00:19:59.820
Train the replacement.

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:04.500
We just had to do that recently ourselves,
and there was a process of training the replacement.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:07.961
If there isn't a training for the replacement,
you're going to be out with somebody that

00:20:07.961 --> 00:20:10.320
needs to know what they're doing for that
two to four-week period, and you don't want

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:11.749
to be doing that, right?

00:20:11.749 --> 00:20:15.529
Or if it's somebody that you just hired and
it's only been a month, you can fire them

00:20:15.529 --> 00:20:18.240
because they can't train the next person anyway,
right?

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:22.799
So it's very, very important, very, very important
for you to know also that you have a replacement

00:20:22.799 --> 00:20:23.799
in mind.

00:20:23.799 --> 00:20:28.880
So, with that being said, this is probably
not the most exciting subject, but many of

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:33.590
you keep asking me about how I fire people,
and how you can go out there and fire someone

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:36.360
properly, as an employee or team member.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:39.559
This is how I've done it over the years and
it's gotten better and better over the years.

00:20:39.559 --> 00:20:42.770
It becomes very clear once you have a philosophy
in place of how you do it.

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:43.940
It becomes very easy.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:48.000
But the key is, when you're building a team,
if you don't build a team full of talented

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.130
people, you're constantly going to be firing
a lot of people if they don't really know

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:52.340
what they're doing.

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:56.340
Hire properly and you won't have to fire too
often because it's never pretty.

00:20:56.340 --> 00:20:57.340
It's very annoying.

